    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.complete_to_chordal_graph - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.complete_to_chordal_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.complete_to_chordal_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Add a minimal set of edges that makes the graph chordal (minimal\n    triangulation).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.complete_to_chordal_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Add a minimal set of edges that makes the graph chordal (minimal\n    triangulation).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.complete_to_chordal_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Add a minimal set of edges that makes the graph chordal (minimal\n  triangulation).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd a minimal set of edges that makes the graph chordal (minimal triangulation).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L34237-L34338){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.complete_to_chordal_graph()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Complete to chordal graph](../assets/graphs/complete-to-chordal-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a five-node cycle 1 to 5, which has no chords and so is not chordal. Right: the returned graph keeps every cycle edge and adds two fill edges in the accent color, 2 to 5 and 3 to 5, cutting the cycle into three triangles. Filled badges give the elimination numbering alpha: node 1 is 5, node 2 is 4, node 5 is 3, node 3 is 2, and node 4 is 1.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nEvery graph can be made chordal by adding *fill* edges. This method adds an inclusion-\nminimal set of them (no fill edge can be dropped while staying chordal), which keeps the\ntriangulation close to the original graph. Triangulating is the first step of building a\njunction tree for exact inference in Bayesian and Markov networks, of ordering sparse\nCholesky factorizations, and of computing tree decompositions.\n\nParallel edges count once and self-loops are ignored when searching; the original edges\nare all kept.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [tuple[Graph, dict[int, int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph with every node and edge of this one plus the fill edges, and the\n    elimination numbering `alpha` (node ID to a position from `n` down to `1`, in the\n    order the nodes were visited). Eliminating nodes in increasing `alpha` adds no\n    further edges.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is directed.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nMCS-M (Berry, Blair, Heggernes and Peyton, 2004) visits nodes by largest weight; when\nnode `z` is visited, every unvisited `y` that can be reached from `z` through unvisited\nnodes of smaller weight than `y` gets its weight increased, and becomes a fill neighbor\nof `z` if not already adjacent. Here the reachability test is one bottleneck search per\nvisited node, $O(V E \\log V)$ overall. The fill edges and the numbering match NetworkX's\n`complete_to_chordal_graph` (which returns all-zero numbers for an already chordal\ngraph; EdgeCase always returns the ordering).\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_chordal`) \n\n:   Check that a graph is chordal.\n\n[](`~junction_tree`) \n\n:   Build a junction tree, triangulating first.\n\n[](`~find_induced_nodes`) \n\n:   The nodes to join when an edge is added.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA five-cycle needs two chords to become chordal:\n\n::: {#11bcdc87 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nring = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(5)\nchordal, alpha = ring.complete_to_chordal_graph()\n\nchordal.count_edges() - ring.count_edges(), chordal.is_chordal()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(2, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Triangulating a conflict graph\n\n`exams()` joins two of a made-up college's courses when some student takes both, so\ntheir exams can't share a time slot. It isn't chordal, and making it so takes 11 fill\nedges. They grow the largest clique from four courses to six, so the triangulation has\ntreewidth 5:\n\n::: {#50b0c3fe .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams().layout_kamada_kawai()\nchordal, alpha = exams.complete_to_chordal_graph()\n\n(\n    chordal.count_edges() - exams.count_edges(),\n    exams.clique_number(),\n    chordal.clique_number(),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(11, 4, 6)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe new graph keeps every node and edge, with their attributes, and adds the fill edges\nafter them. They have no `shared` count, which picks them out:\n\n::: {#edb6f4c9 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfill = [\n    row[\"id\"]\n    for row in chordal.get_edge_df().iter_rows(named=True)\n    if row[\"shared\"] is None\n]\nchordal.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight_edges=fill, label_by=\"label\", height=420\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%;